# a type narrowed to a disjunction of some of its values: no single new
# alternative covers the prior one, and a value of it that new rejects is a
# counterexample ("" for string, "x" where "" is taken, 2 for int)
-- old.cue --
kind: string
e!:   string
i?:   int
-- new.cue --
kind: "a" | "b"
e!:   "" | "a"
i?:   0 | 1
-- out --
incompatible
e is now "" | "a", which does not accept every value of the prior string (rule value-narrowed) @ new.cue:2:1
i is now 0 | 1, which does not accept every value of the prior int (rule value-narrowed) @ new.cue:3:1
kind is now "a" | "b", which does not accept every value of the prior string (rule value-narrowed) @ new.cue:1:1
